<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="sec-u2-linear-independence">
  <title>Linear independence</title>

  <activity>
    <title>Comparing two spanning sets (U2-LO4)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="act-5-1-W-proper-subspace"/> we showed that the set
        <me> W = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} r \\ s \\ r + 2s \end{bmatrix} : r,s \in \R \right\} </me>
        is a subspace.
        In <xref ref="act-5-1-W-Span"/>, we showed that the set
        <me>S = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</me>
        is a spanning set for <m>W</m>. The set
        <me>T = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</me>
        also spans <m>W</m>. But why is <m>S</m> <sq>better</sq>?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        It uses fewer vectors to span the same space. Which vectors in <m>T</m> are redundant?
        <me>
          \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
          =
          2\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 3 \end{bmatrix}
          =
          \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
          +
          \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        So <m>T</m> spans <m>W</m>, but it contains repeated information.
      </p>
      <p>
        For example, the sum of the first three vectors listed in <m>T</m> can be rewritten as
        <me>
          \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
          + \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 0 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
          + \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
          = 3\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
          + \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        The right-hand side is a linear combination of the two vectors in <m>S</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <p>
    A subspace <m>U</m> has many different spanning sets. We use the concept of <em>linear independence</em> to determine when sets of spanning vectors are the <sq>most efficient</sq>. In data terms, independence asks whether one feature direction is already built from the others.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-5-2-linear-indepdendence">
    <title>Linear Independence</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A set of vectors <m>\{ \mathbf{x}_1, \dots, \mathbf{x}_k \}</m> in <m>\R^n</m> is said to be <term>linearly independent</term>, or simply <term>independent</term> if the only solution to the equation <m>t_1 \mathbf{x}_1 + \dots + t_k \mathbf{x}_k = \mathbf{0}</m> is trivial, i.e., <m>t_1 = \cdots = t_k = 0</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        A set of vectors <m>\{ \mathbf{x}_1, \dots, \mathbf{x}_k \}</m> in <m>\R^n</m> is <term>linearly dependent</term> if it is not linearly independent, i.e., if there exists scalars <m>t_1,\dots,t_k</m>, not all zero, such that <m>t_1 \mathbf{x}_1 + \cdots + t_k \mathbf{x}_k = \mathbf{0}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark xml:id="rem-u2-zero-vector-dependent">
    <title>An independent list cannot contain the zero vector</title>
    <p>
      Any list containing <m>\mathbf{0}</m> is linearly dependent. Give the zero vector coefficient <m>1</m> and every other vector coefficient <m>0</m>:
      <me>1\cdot\mathbf{0}+0\mathbf{x}_2+\cdots+0\mathbf{x}_k=\mathbf{0}.</me>
      This linear combination equals the zero vector, but its coefficients are not all zero.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <activity xml:id="act-u2-independence-two-vectors">
    <title>Independence of two vectors (U2-LO4)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{u}</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}</m> be two nonzero vectors in <m>\R^n</m>. Explain why <m>\{\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}\}</m> is linearly independent if and only if neither vector is a scalar multiple of the other.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        If one vector is a scalar multiple of the other, say <m>\mathbf{v}=c\mathbf{u}</m>, then
        <me>c\mathbf{u}-\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{0}.</me>
        The coefficients <m>c</m> and <m>-1</m> are not both zero, so the vectors are linearly dependent. The same reasoning applies if <m>\mathbf{u}</m> is a scalar multiple of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Conversely, suppose the vectors are linearly dependent. Then
        <me>s\mathbf{u}+t\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{0}</me>
        for scalars <m>s</m> and <m>t</m> that are not both zero. We must have <m>t\ne 0</m>: otherwise <m>s\mathbf{u}=\mathbf{0}</m>, and since <m>\mathbf{u}\ne\mathbf{0}</m>, this would force <m>s=0</m> as well. Therefore
        <me>\mathbf{v}=-\frac{s}{t}\mathbf{u},</me>
        so one vector is a scalar multiple of the other. Thus, if neither vector is a scalar multiple of the other, the only solution is <m>s=t=0</m>, and the vectors are linearly independent.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <example xml:id="sec-u2-linear-independence-5">
    <title>Testing independence in three dimensions (U2-LO4)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether the vectors
        <me>
          \mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 2 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix} \quad \mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 3 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \quad \mathbf{v}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 4 \\ -2 \end{bmatrix}
        </me>
        are linearly independent.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We need to determine the solutions to the equation <m>t_1 \mathbf{v}_1 + t_2 \mathbf{v}_2 + t_3 \mathbf{v}_3 = \mathbf{0}</m>. Expanding out this equation gives the system
        <md>
          <mrow>
            3t_1 + t_2 - t_3 \amp = 0
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2t_1 + 3t_2 + 4t_3 \amp = 0
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2t_1 + 0t_2 - 2t_3 \amp = 0
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <url href="https://math345-games.github.io/TheEliminationScratchpad/the_elimination_scratchpad.html?v=1&amp;activity=2.4.5&amp;m=3%2C1%2C-1%2C0~2%2C3%2C4%2C0~2%2C0%2C-2%2C0&amp;vars=t_1%2Ct_2%2Ct_3&amp;goal=classify&amp;back=https%3A%2F%2Fmath345-textbook.github.io%2Fsec-u2-independence-bases-dimension.html%23sec-u2-linear-independence-5" visual="The Elimination Scratchpad">Practice this system in The Elimination Scratchpad</url>
      </p>
      <p>
        We solve this homogeneous system of linear equations using the Gaussian elimination algorithm. First we create the augmented matrix
        <me>
          \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
          3 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
          2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 0 \\
          2 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0
          \end{array}\right]
        </me>
        We reduce the matrix to row echelon form, scaling each pivot row to obtain a leading one before eliminating entries below it:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \amp \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            3 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
            2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 0 \\
            2 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0
            \end{array}\right]
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp \xrightarrow{R_1\to \frac{1}{3}R_1}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1 \amp \frac{1}{3} \amp -\frac{1}{3} \amp 0 \\
            2 \amp 3 \amp 4 \amp 0 \\
            2 \amp 0 \amp -2 \amp 0
            \end{array}\right]
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp \xrightarrow{\substack{R_2\to R_2-2R_1\\R_3\to R_3-2R_1}}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1 \amp \frac{1}{3} \amp -\frac{1}{3} \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp \frac{7}{3} \amp \frac{14}{3} \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp -\frac{2}{3} \amp -\frac{4}{3} \amp 0
            \end{array}\right]
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp \xrightarrow{R_2\to \frac{3}{7}R_2}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1 \amp \frac{1}{3} \amp -\frac{1}{3} \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp -\frac{2}{3} \amp -\frac{4}{3} \amp 0
            \end{array}\right]
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp \xrightarrow{R_3\to R_3+\frac{2}{3}R_2}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1 \amp \frac{1}{3} \amp -\frac{1}{3} \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{array}\right]
          </mrow>
        </md>
        There is one free variable corresponding to this matrix, which implies that there is one basic solution to the equation, and thus the system has a nontrivial solution. Thus the vectors are <em>not</em> linearly independent.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <activity xml:id="act-u2-dependent-nonparallel-vectors">
    <title>Testing independence of three nonparallel vectors (U2-LO4)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use Gaussian elimination to determine whether the vectors
        <me>
          \mathbf{v}_1=\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\1\end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          \mathbf{v}_2=\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\\1\end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          \mathbf{v}_3=\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\2\end{bmatrix}
        </me>
        are linearly independent. If they are dependent, give a nontrivial linear combination of them that equals <m>\mathbf{0}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <url href="https://math345-games.github.io/TheEliminationScratchpad/the_elimination_scratchpad.html?v=1&amp;activity=2.4.6&amp;m=1%2C0%2C1%2C0~0%2C1%2C1%2C0~1%2C1%2C2%2C0&amp;vars=t_1%2Ct_2%2Ct_3&amp;goal=classify&amp;back=https%3A%2F%2Fmath345-textbook.github.io%2Fsec-u2-independence-bases-dimension.html%23act-u2-dependent-nonparallel-vectors" visual="The Elimination Scratchpad">Practice this activity in The Elimination Scratchpad</url>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We determine whether <m>t_1\mathbf{v}_1+t_2\mathbf{v}_2+t_3\mathbf{v}_3=\mathbf{0}</m> has a nontrivial solution. Comparing coordinates gives
        <md>
          <mrow>t_1+t_3\amp=0</mrow>
          <mrow>t_2+t_3\amp=0</mrow>
          <mrow>t_1+t_2+2t_3\amp=0.</mrow>
        </md>
        The first two pivots are already <m>1</m>, so we only need to eliminate the entries below them:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \amp\left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1\amp0\amp1\amp0\\
            0\amp1\amp1\amp0\\
            1\amp1\amp2\amp0
            \end{array}\right]
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp\xrightarrow{R_3\to R_3-R_1}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1\amp0\amp1\amp0\\
            0\amp1\amp1\amp0\\
            0\amp1\amp1\amp0
            \end{array}\right]
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp\xrightarrow{R_3\to R_3-R_2}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1\amp0\amp1\amp0\\
            0\amp1\amp1\amp0\\
            0\amp0\amp0\amp0
            \end{array}\right].
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        The variable <m>t_3</m> is free. Setting <m>t_3=s</m> gives <m>t_1=-s</m> and <m>t_2=-s</m>. In particular, choosing <m>s=1</m> gives the nontrivial relation
        <me>-\mathbf{v}_1-\mathbf{v}_2+\mathbf{v}_3=\mathbf{0}.</me>
        Thus the three vectors are linearly dependent, and <m>\mathbf{v}_3=\mathbf{v}_1+\mathbf{v}_2</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <warning xml:id="warn-u2-nonparallel-not-independent">
    <title>Nonparallel vectors can still be dependent</title>
    <p>
      In <xref ref="act-u2-dependent-nonparallel-vectors"/>, the third vector is not parallel to either of the first two: its second entry is <m>1</m>, while every scalar multiple of <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> has second entry <m>0</m>; its first entry is <m>1</m>, while every scalar multiple of <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> has first entry <m>0</m>. The first two vectors are also not parallel. Nevertheless, the three vectors are dependent because <m>\mathbf{v}_3=\mathbf{v}_1+\mathbf{v}_2</m>. For three or more vectors, checking that no pair is parallel is not enough to establish linear independence: a vector can be a linear combination of the others without being a scalar multiple of any one of them.
    </p>
  </warning>

  <p xml:id="sec-u2-linear-independence-6">
    The preceding example and activity demonstrate an algorithm to verify whether a given set of vectors is linearly independent.
  </p>

  <algorithm xml:id="sec-u2-linear-independence-7">
    <title>A Test of Independence</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        To verify if a set <m>\{ \mathbf{x}_1,\dots,\mathbf{x}_k \}</m> in <m>\R^n</m> is linearly independent:
        <ol>
          <li>
            Consider the system of linear equations <m>t_1 \mathbf{x}_1 + \cdots + t_k \mathbf{x}_k = \mathbf{0}</m>.
          </li>

          <li>
            Solve the resulting homogeneous linear system.
          </li>

          <li>
            If there is a nontrivial solution to the equation, then the vectors are linearly dependent. Otherwise, the vectors are linearly independent.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </algorithm>

  <p xml:id="sec-u2-linear-independence-8">
    In matrix form: put the vectors <m>\mathbf{x}_1,\dots,\mathbf{x}_k</m> as the columns of a matrix <m>M</m>. Then <m>\{ \mathbf{x}_1,\dots,\mathbf{x}_k \}</m> is independent exactly when <m>M\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{0}</m> has only the trivial solution, equivalently when every column of <m>M</m> is a pivot column.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="sec-u2-linear-independence-9">
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>\{ \mathbf{x}_1, \dots, \mathbf{x}_k \}</m> is a linearly independent set of vectors in <m>\R^n</m>, then every vector in their span, <m>\spans \{ \mathbf{x}_1, \dots, \mathbf{x}_k \}</m>, has a unique representation as a linear combination of the <m>\mathbf{x}_i</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{x}</m> be an element of <m>\spans \{ \mathbf{x}_1, \dots, \mathbf{x}_k \}</m>. If the equation
        <me>\mathbf{x} = a_1 \mathbf{x}_1 + \cdots + a_k \mathbf{x}_k</me>
        had another solution, i.e.,
        <me>\mathbf{x} = b_1 \mathbf{x}_1 + \cdots + b_k \mathbf{x}_k</me>,
        then subtracting one equation from the other gives that
        <me>(a_1 - b_1) \mathbf{x}_1 + \cdots + (a_k - b_k) \mathbf{x}_k = 0 </me>.
        Thus the coefficients <m>a_1 - b_1, \dots, a_k - b_k</m> give a solution to the equation
        <me>t_1 \mathbf{x}_1 + \cdots + t_k \mathbf{x}_k = 0</me>.
        But the vectors <m>\mathbf{x}_1,\dots,\mathbf{x}_k</m> are linearly independent, and so this equation has only a trivial solution. Thus <m>a_1 - b_1, \dots, a_k - b_k = 0</m>, i.e., <m>a_1 = b_1, \dots, a_k = b_k</m>, proving that the representation of <m>\mathbf{x}</m> as a linear combination is unique.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <activity xml:id="sec-u2-linear-independence-10">
    <title>Testing independence in four dimensions (U2-LO4)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether the vectors
        <me>
          \mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \quad \mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -2 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}\quad \mathbf{v}_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ -3 \\ -1 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix}
        </me>
        are linearly independent.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We must check if the equation
        <me>t_1 \mathbf{v}_1 + t_2 \mathbf{v}_2 + t_3 \mathbf{v}_3 = 0</me>
        has nontrivial solutions. Expanding out this equation gives the system
        <md>
          <mrow>
            t_1 + t_2 \amp = 0
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2t_1 - 2t_2 - 3t_3 \amp = 0
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            -t_3 \amp = 0
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            t_1 - t_3 \amp = 0
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <url href="https://math345-games.github.io/TheEliminationScratchpad/the_elimination_scratchpad.html?v=1&amp;activity=2.4.10&amp;m=1%2C1%2C0%2C0~2%2C-2%2C-3%2C0~0%2C0%2C-1%2C0~1%2C0%2C-1%2C0&amp;vars=t_1%2Ct_2%2Ct_3&amp;goal=classify&amp;back=https%3A%2F%2Fmath345-textbook.github.io%2Fsec-u2-independence-bases-dimension.html%23sec-u2-linear-independence-10" visual="The Elimination Scratchpad">Practice this system in The Elimination Scratchpad</url>
      </p>
      <p>
        We first apply the Gaussian elimination algorithm to the augmented matrix. The first pivot is already a leading one, so we eliminate below it, then scale row 2 and eliminate below its leading one:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \amp \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            2 \amp -2 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
            1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0
            \end{array}\right]
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp \xrightarrow{\substack{R_2\to R_2-2R_1\\R_4\to R_4-R_1}}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp -4 \amp -3 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 0
            \end{array}\right]
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp \xrightarrow{R_2\to -\frac{1}{4}R_2}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp \frac{3}{4} \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 0
            \end{array}\right]
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp \xrightarrow{R_4\to R_4+R_2}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp \frac{3}{4} \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp -\frac{1}{4} \amp 0
            \end{array}\right]
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        For the remaining rows, scale row 3 and eliminate the entry below its leading one:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \amp \xrightarrow{R_3\to -R_3}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp \frac{3}{4} \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp -\frac{1}{4} \amp 0
            \end{array}\right]
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \amp \xrightarrow{R_4\to R_4+\frac{1}{4}R_3}
            \left[\begin{array}{ccc|c}
            1 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 1 \amp \frac{3}{4} \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
            0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{array}\right].
          </mrow>
        </md>
        Back substitution gives <m>t_3=0</m>, then <m>t_2=0</m>, and finally <m>t_1=0</m>. Thus the only solution is the trivial solution, and the vectors are linearly independent.
      </p>
      <p>
        We could have reached this conclusion faster by inspecting the original equations. The third equation immediately gives <m>t_3=0</m>, the fourth then gives <m>t_1=0</m>, and the first gives <m>t_2=0</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

</subsection>
